<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1310–<a href="/years/1367">1367</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Cardinal Albornoz, Gil de Albornoz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz was a Castilian archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and cardinal who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> popes as legate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. From 1353 he reconquered the Papal States from local lords and city tyrants by war and diplomacy, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egidian-constitutions">Egidian Constitutions</a> of 1357 gave them a lasting legal framework. He founded the Spanish College at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz.jpg" alt="Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz" width="222" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albornoz_detail,_Bonaiuto_fresco.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albornoz was born in Carrascosa del Campo, in Cuenca. His father, Garcialuarez Albornoz, fourth lord of Albornoz, was a tutor to the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile">Alfonso XI of Castile</a>, and his mother, Doña Teresa de Luna, was a sister of Jimeno de Luna, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. He was raised and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> with his mother's brother and studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. The cathedral chapter of Toledo elected him to succeed his uncle as archbishop, and Pope Benedict XII confirmed the election on 13 May 1338.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As archbishop he held two reform synods, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in May 1339 and at Alcalá in April 1347. He fought against a Marinid invasion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a> in 1340 and led the armed levy of his archbishopric at the taking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> in 1344. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on the death of Alfonso XI in March 1350 and never returned; the claim that he fled for fear of Pedro of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> rests on no contemporary evidence. In December 1350 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> made him cardinal-priest of San Clemente, and he resigned Toledo.</p><p>Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as legate in 1353 at the head of a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-di-vico">Giovanni di Vico</a>, lord of Viterbo, at the battle of Viterbo on 10 March 1354. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> the Malatesta of Rimini, the Montefeltro of Urbino and the da Polenta of Ravenna submitted. After a recall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> he returned to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-ii-ordelaffi">Francesco II Ordelaffi</a> of Forlì, who had hired Konrad von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a>'s Grand Company, and Ordelaffi surrendered on 4 July 1359. When Innocent VI died, Albornoz refused the papal tiara and Urban V was elected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The decade of war did not pacify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Mercenary companies still roamed the peninsula, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velletri">Velletri</a> from 1361 to 1367, and Campagna rose in a general rebellion, while papal forces executed the whole population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a>, a town loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. In 1364 Albornoz founded the College of Saint Clement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> for Castilian, Aragonese and Portuguese students. Urban V made him legate at Bologna in 1367, but he died at Viterbo the same year. At his wish his remains were carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, where Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> had them entombed with almost royal honours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Cardinal Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz</b> reconquered the papal provinces in 1353–1367 with mercenary armies and a ring of fortresses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1354 <b>Cardinal Albornoz</b> restored papal authority, and Orvieto remained part of the Papal States.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-orvieto">Commune of Orvieto</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 14th century Giovanni di Vico made himself lord of the city until <b>Cardinal Albornoz</b> restored papal authority in 1354.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-viterbo">Commune of Viterbo</a></li><li><blockquote>Francesco II Ordelaffi resisted <b>Cardinal Albornoz's</b> campaign to restore papal rule for years; his wife Cia degli Ubaldini defended Cesena in 1357 until forced to surrender, and Forlì fell in 1359.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-forli">Lordship of Forli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Carrascosa del Campo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Viterbo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.04,-2.74&amp;year=1347" aria-label="Carrascosa del Campo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4821 4144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4821 4144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.04,-2.74&amp;year=1347">What was here in 1347? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
